  <section id="how" aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <h2 id="how-h">How Steps Convert to Distance</h2>
    <p class="tp-note" style="font-size:17px!important"><span class="tp-kbd">distance = steps &times; step length</span></p><p>Step length is estimated as 41.5% of height for men and 41.3% for women when walking, and about 1.65 times that when running. Measure your own with the <a href="/stride-length-calculator/">stride length calculator</a> for a closer result. Calories use standard MET values: about 3.5 for brisk walking and 9.8 for running at 6 mph.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="goal" aria-labelledby="goal-h">
    <h2 id="goal-h">Is 10,000 Steps a Day Enough?</h2>
    <p>The 10,000-step goal began as a 1960s Japanese marketing slogan, but research supports the idea: in large studies, the risk of early death falls steadily up to about 7,000&ndash;10,000 steps a day for adults, with smaller gains beyond. Older adults see most of the benefit by 6,000&ndash;8,000 steps. Any increase from a low baseline helps.</p>
  </section>

